The attached image illustrates the in vivo kinetics and biodistribution of Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 nanoparticles after intramuscular injection in mice. Panel A shows that Nano-11 is initially retained at the injection site and gradually clears over 21 days, with Panel C quantifying this decrease in fluorescence intensity over time. Panel B, depicting excised organs at day 21, confirms that Nano-11 primarily remains localized to the injection site and draining lymph nodes, demonstrating minimal systemic distribution to other internal organs and supporting a favorable safety profile with transient inflammation.

The image, combined with the PDF's findings, significantly contributes to cancer research applications by demonstrating Nano-11's potential as a nanocarrier for cancer vaccines. Its ability to efficiently deliver antigens to migratory dendritic cells and induce a localized immune response makes it an excellent candidate for targeted delivery of tumor-associated antigens or neoantigens. This could lead to the development of more potent anti-tumor immune responses, while the favorable biodistribution profile suggests a safer alternative to conventional adjuvants in cancer immunotherapy.